1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,185
On the exam, you work with the documentation already installed on the machine.

2
00:00:05,205 --> 00:00:09,127
Knowing how to find the right page, fast, is a skill of its own.

3
00:00:09,597 --> 00:00:10,738
In this video:

4
00:00:10,758 --> 00:00:16,044
locate, read, and use system documentation, including man, info,

5
00:00:16,064 --> 00:00:18,366
and files in /usr/share/doc.

6
00:00:22,886 --> 00:00:25,469
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

7
00:00:25,939 --> 00:00:31,369
Locate, read, and use system documentation including man, info,

8
00:00:31,389 --> 00:00:33,791
and files in /usr/share/doc.

9
00:00:34,261 --> 00:00:36,698
We'll search for the right page, read it,

10
00:00:36,718 --> 00:00:39,395
fix two things missing on this fresh machine,

11
00:00:39,415 --> 00:00:41,793
and then find extra documentation files.

12
00:00:49,313 --> 00:00:53,381
Say you need to change a file's owner, but you forget the command.

13
00:00:53,401 --> 00:00:58,274
man -k searches page descriptions for a keyword. We search for owner and group.

14
00:01:03,744 --> 00:01:08,430
Nothing appropriate. On a fresh machine, the search index is not built yet.

15
00:01:08,450 --> 00:01:12,258
whatis, which looks up a command by name, fails the same way.

16
00:01:15,228 --> 00:01:16,905
mandb builds the index.

17
00:01:16,925 --> 00:01:21,997
It needs sudo, because it writes system files, and -q keeps it quiet.

18
00:01:22,017 --> 00:01:26,130
Now the search finds chown: change file owner and group.

19
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:36,276
whatis shows the one-line summary, with the section number in brackets.

20
00:01:36,296 --> 00:01:37,532
ls is in section 1.

21
00:01:42,052 --> 00:01:49,200
Now read a page. man -w prints where the page file lives, under /usr/share/man.

22
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:58,515
man ls opens the page in a pager called less. At the top:

23
00:01:58,535 --> 00:02:03,937
the NAME, the SYNOPSIS, and the DESCRIPTION. The bottom line shows where you are.

24
00:02:09,407 --> 00:02:11,118
Space moves down one screen.

25
00:02:11,138 --> 00:02:15,382
The options come next, alphabetically, each with a short explanation.

26
00:02:18,852 --> 00:02:20,520
A slash starts a search.

27
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:26,005
We type /human and press Enter, and it jumps to the human-readable option, -h.

28
00:02:26,025 --> 00:02:27,131
Press q to quit.

29
00:02:34,651 --> 00:02:37,774
Pages are grouped into numbered sections.

30
00:02:37,794 --> 00:02:41,531
Section 1 is commands, section 5 is file formats.

31
00:02:41,551 --> 00:02:47,587
passwd is both a command and a file, /etc/passwd. man passwd gives the command.

32
00:02:47,607 --> 00:02:51,420
For the file, put the section first: man 5 passwd.

33
00:02:57,890 --> 00:03:00,948
Here, section 5 for passwd is missing,

34
00:03:00,968 --> 00:03:07,508
because the man-pages package is not installed. dnf install -y man-pages adds it.

35
00:03:07,528 --> 00:03:10,585
The download output is shortened here.

36
00:03:21,105 --> 00:03:24,957
whatis still shows two entries, because the index is old.

37
00:03:24,977 --> 00:03:28,354
We rebuild it with mandb, and now there are three:

38
00:03:28,374 --> 00:03:32,498
the command in section 1, and the password file in section 5.

39
00:03:40,018 --> 00:03:45,065
man -w with 5 finds the page file, and man 5 passwd opens it:

40
00:03:45,085 --> 00:03:49,385
the password file, and a description of /etc/passwd.

41
00:03:55,905 --> 00:03:58,298
info is the other manual format.

42
00:03:58,318 --> 00:04:03,425
It isn't installed here either, so the shell says command not found.

43
00:04:03,445 --> 00:04:05,762
dnf install -y info fixes that.

44
00:04:14,282 --> 00:04:17,060
info ls opens the GNU manual for ls.

45
00:04:17,080 --> 00:04:22,035
It reads more like a book, organized in sections you can follow.

46
00:04:22,055 --> 00:04:24,289
Press q to quit, as with man.

47
00:04:30,809 --> 00:04:33,551
The third place is /usr/share/doc:

48
00:04:33,571 --> 00:04:39,642
one folder per installed package, holding extras like README and FAQ files.

49
00:04:41,112 --> 00:04:44,157
ls on that folder, piped into head -n 15.

50
00:04:44,177 --> 00:04:50,138
The pipe sends the output of ls to head, and head shows only the first 15 lines.

51
00:04:57,658 --> 00:05:01,414
Inside the chrony folder there is a README, an FAQ, and more.

52
00:05:03,884 --> 00:05:10,179
rpm -qd lists the documentation files of a package, including its man pages.

53
00:05:17,699 --> 00:05:21,263
And head -n 8 shows the first eight lines of the README.

54
00:05:28,783 --> 00:05:32,219
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

55
00:05:33,739 --> 00:05:36,658
The index lives on disk, so it survives a reboot.

56
00:05:36,678 --> 00:05:39,116
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

57
00:05:42,586 --> 00:05:45,660
After a fresh boot, the same search still finds chown.

58
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:55,661
Your turn. Create a folder lab11.

59
00:05:55,681 --> 00:06:00,284
Use the manual to find the ls option that sorts by file size,

60
00:06:00,304 --> 00:06:04,906
and the section number for the format of the /etc/group file.

61
00:06:04,926 --> 00:06:08,620
Write the option on line one of lab11/answer.txt,

62
00:06:08,640 --> 00:06:11,272
and the section number on line two.

63
00:06:16,742 --> 00:06:20,669
Check with cat. Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

64
00:06:26,189 --> 00:06:30,068
That finishes the Essential tools objectives. Next up:

65
00:06:30,088 --> 00:06:32,740
Configure access to RPM repositories.

66
00:06:33,210 --> 00:06:36,112
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
